      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by their quoted evidence. Tier assignments are appropriate: USO cost figures and losses are measurable (Ofcom/regulated data); savings projections are correctly labelled projected. The E11 stamp-price claim (eragroup.com, a commercial source) is used to support a claim that Royal Mail itself attributed price rises to USO pressures \u2014 this is a mild sourcing concern but not blocking since it quotes Royal Mail's own attribution. The E16 polling claim is accurately represented. The 'Ofcom concluded the existing USO is financially unsustainable' claim is labelled projected \u2014 arguably this is a regulatory finding (measurable), but labelling it projected is conservative and not wrong. Direction 'worsens' at minor/long-term is defensible: the surviving claims coherently support that maintaining a structurally loss-making obligation foregoes savings and continues cost pass-through to users. No party identity or motive attribution detected. No false balance issue \u2014 the verdict does not reach mixed/too-uncertain. Employment and rural connectivity are appropriately redirected to other outcomes. Verdict passes."
